  "note": "States that tax some Social Security benefits in 2026 (West Virginia finished its phase-out in 2026). All other states exempt it. Colorado exempts it in full from 65 (modelled). The others exempt it below an income limit, not modelled: CT (AGI under $75,000 single, $100,000 joint), MN (a subtraction that phases out above about $82,000 single, $105,000 joint), MT (taxed like the federal rules, with a senior deduction), NM (under $100,000 single, $150,000 joint), RI (from full retirement age, under about $104,000 single, $130,000 joint), UT (a credit that phases out above $45,000 single, $75,000 joint), VT (under $50,000 single, $65,000 joint). So the plan may overstate state tax on Social Security there. Source: Tax Foundation, 'Does Your State Tax Social Security Benefits?' (2025) and state revenue pages; limits rounded.",
  "url": "https://taxfoundation.org/data/all/state/states-that-tax-social-security-benefits/",
